<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mikhail Terekhov<sup id="id1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1,2</span></sup><sup id="id1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">*</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Alexander Panfilov<sup id="id1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3,4</span></sup><sup id="id1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">*</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Daniil Dzenhaliou<sup id="id1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup><sup id="id1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">*</span></sup></span></span></span>
<span id="id1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="id1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Caglar Gulcehre<sup id="id1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maksym Andriushchenko<sup id="id1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3,4†</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ameya Prabhu<sup id="id1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">4,5†</span></sup></span>  <span id="id1.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jonas Geiping<sup id="id1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3,4†</span></sup></span></span></span>
</span>
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="id2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="id2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="id2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup>MATS <sup id="id2.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>EPFL <sup id="id2.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3</span></sup>ELLIS Institute Tübingen &amp; Max Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems</span>
<span id="id2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="id2.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">4</span></sup>Tübingen AI Center <sup id="id2.2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">5</span></sup>University of Tübingen</span>
</span>
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">AI control protocols serve as a defense mechanism to stop untrusted LLM agents from causing harm in autonomous settings. Prior work treats this as a security problem, stress testing with exploits that use the deployment context to subtly complete harmful side tasks, such as backdoor insertion. In practice, most AI control protocols are fundamentally based on LLM monitors, which can become a central point of failure. We study <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adaptive</span> attacks by an untrusted model that knows the protocol and the monitor model, which is plausible if the untrusted model was trained with a later knowledge cutoff or can search for this information autonomously. We instantiate a simple adaptive attack vector by which the attacker embeds publicly known or zero-shot prompt injections in the model outputs. Using this tactic, frontier models consistently evade diverse monitors and complete malicious tasks on two main AI control benchmarks. The attack works universally against current protocols that rely on a monitor. Furthermore, the recent Defer-to-Resample protocol even backfires, as its resampling amplifies the prompt injection and effectively reframes it as a best-of-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> attack. In general, adaptive attacks on monitor models represent a major blind spot in current control protocols and should become a standard component of evaluations for future AI control mechanisms.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>Equal contribution, order decided by dice roll. Correspondence to <a href="mailto:mikhail.terekhov@epfl.ch" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">mikhail.terekhov@epfl.ch</a>.</span></span></span><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="footnotex2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>Equal supervision.</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h3>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Background.</span>  
The AI control problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>)</cite> focuses on designing protocols that oversee advanced AI systems that can intentionally subvert safety measures. Control protocols typically rely on a monitor: a <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trusted</span> model that scores, blocks, or rewrites outputs of the target <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">untrusted</span> model.
Importantly, the monitor model is assumed to be weaker than the untrusted model, so monitoring can be seen as an instance of the broader <span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">scalable oversight</span> problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bowman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
As we demonstrate, the reliance on a monitor creates a single point of failure: if the untrusted model misleads or hijacks the monitor, the control protocols fail at their core. Most prior work stress-tests protocols against intentionally <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subtle</em> misbehavior, where a subversive AI model presents its actions as benign using the semantics of the task it is working on. For example, the untrusted model might embed backdoors in code that disguise themselves as legitimate functionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, or orchestrate multi-step attacks in agentic workflows where individual actions appear benign in isolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, these attacks typically do not exploit a critical part of control protocols: the monitor that approves or rejects generated content is a <span id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weaker</span>, static model, that has to contend with a <span id="S1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stronger</span>, more recent model that has to be assumed to have knowledge of the AI control protocol that is being deployed. In our work, we show that untrusted models can execute adaptive attacks that directly compromise the monitor through prompt injections embedded in the untrusted model’s outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our setting.</span>  
Following Kerckhoffs’s principle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon, 1949</a>)</cite>, we assume that <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">security through obscurity</span> is insufficient, and the red team’s subversive AI <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">knows</span> the control protocol: the monitor’s identity and the algorithms in the protocol, including the decision thresholds. Kerckhoffs’s principle has a long history in the security literature, in machine learning particularly in the context of adversarial robustness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tramer et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
In the context of AI control, it is justified by the fact that frontier models often know they are being evaluated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laine et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdelnabi &amp; Salem, 2025</a>)</cite>, defense protocols are openly documented,
attackers may have a later knowledge cutoff than the monitor and may significantly exceed its capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This problem is exacerbated if the AI agent can retrieve the latest jailbreaks using web search.
Leveraging the later knowledge cutoff of the untrusted model, we craft an exploit: a prompt injection embedded within the untrusted model’s outputs, specifically tailored to the monitor’s identity. We further argue that this attack vector is critical to understanding the worst-case risk of a control protocol.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our contributions.</span>  
We present our experiments in two scenarios.
First, the untrusted model plants an LLM-generated prompt injection that we produce by adapting a publicly available jailbreak using DeepSeek-Chat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Using a compliant model like DeepSeek avoids confounding attack potency with the untrusted model’s non-compliance to produce jailbreaks.
Second, the model produces a self-generated injection, which required us to jailbreak the untrusted model beforehand.
The designed injections successfully induce the monitor to assign low suspiciousness scores and green-light malicious outputs, entirely bypassing downstream protocols that begin after potentially malicious content has been flagged. When the attacker-monitor capability gap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al., 2025</a>)</cite> is sufficiently large (e.g., GPT-5 vs. GPT-4o-mini), the untrusted model can effortlessly invent working prompt injections on the spot (see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Prompt Injection Design ‣ 3 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). Our results demonstrate that simple prompt injections can fully subvert existing protocols that rely on a monitor. Furthermore, a single monitor-specific injection template transfers across benchmarks with minimal change, indicating broad cross-protocol applicability.
Worse still, a recent promising AI control approach from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite> backfires in our setting: by sampling until a permissive trajectory appears, it effectively converts a zero-shot prompt injection attempt into a best-of-<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> attack, significantly amplifying attack success rates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hughes et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Overall, our findings suggest that as long as non-robust LLM monitors remain the primary oversight tool, AI control protocols can be easily compromised.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:218.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/fig_0_v4.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:302/179;" width="302" height="179"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:178.9pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/fig_0_v5.svg" id="S1.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:247/179;" width="247" height="179"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Control and Protocols Subversion.</span>   <span id="S1.F1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> The AI control setup is an auditing game between a red team and a blue team. <span id="S1.F1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> The red team sequentially submits solutions to agentic tasks, some of which are malicious (e.g., backdoored). <span id="S1.F1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> These solutions are audited by the blue team with the assistance of a trusted AI monitor. <span id="S1.F1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> Finally, the blue team applies <em id="S1.F1.19" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">control protocols</em> to decide which solutions should be escalated for costly human auditing. We show that modern AI control protocols can be effectively subverted if the red team’s attack policy includes inserting <em id="S1.F1.20" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt injections</em> into its actions to sabotage the blue team. <span id="S1.F1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom right:</span> Our attack policy significantly increases the proportion of malicious actions by the red team that are classified as benign by three different monitor models. <span id="S1.F1.22" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top right:</span> As a result, the safety-usefulness Pareto frontiers provided by modern AI control protocols <span id="S1.F1.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">collapse</span> to upfront auditing as monitors become ineffective.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Threat Model for AI Control</h3>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since we focus on adaptive attacks on AI control protocols, we find it is important to clearly state the threat model and key assumptions we make. We provide a schematic of the setting in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">General Setting.</span>  
Following  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we frame AI control as a sequential game between a blue team (defender) and a red team (attacker). A game episode may last up to <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> steps. At each step, the environment draws an i.i.d. task from a fixed distribution. The red team attempts to solve the task using a strong <em id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">untrusted model</em>. Solving a task may require multiple interactions with the environment. In parallel, the red team may pursue a <em id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">side task</em> (the <em id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sabotage task</em>), which it aims to complete without detection.
The blue team deploys a <em id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">control protocol</em> that governs its interactions with the red team. It can use a weaker model that scores the suspiciousness of red team’s moves to identify which of the untrusted model’s actions to flag for expensive auditing. We focus on AI control in the <em id="S2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">high-stakes</em> setting, where a single detected malicious action is sufficient for the blue team to win, and a single successful non-audited sabotage is sufficient for the red team to win. Below, we describe what information and affordances each team has access to.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Blue Team.</span>   The blue team commits to its protocol before the game begins.
It has access to a <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trusted model</em>: a weaker but extensively tested safe LLM. The blue team is assumed to have only <span id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">black-box access</span> to the untrusted model: it cannot see the model’s residual stream, its system prompt or chain-of-thought, but it can inspect any user-visible input to the model, any tool calls the model makes, and all model outputs.
Importantly, the blue team is <span id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stateful</span> and observes the full history of the red team’s outputs, which can help to catch a misaligned model.
The blue team also has limited access to expensive, high-quality human reviewers, who can audit a small fraction <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> of the red team’s actions – this is referred to as the <em id="S2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">auditing budget</em>. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terekhov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we assume that a human audit perfectly reveals whether the red team is attempting sabotage at the audited moment. Throughout this paper, we set the auditing budget <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0.02</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2023)</a></cite> and provide a sensitivity analysis of our main results in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Sensitivity to the Auditing Budget 𝑐 ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Red Team.</span>   The red team moves second and adapts its attack to the fixed protocol. Following Kerckhoffs’s principle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon, 1949</a>)</cite>, we assume the red team knows the monitoring model and the prompts, the protocol algorithm, and its thresholds. Many defenses fail once adversarial attacks do not rely on security through obscurity and are tailored to a particular defense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tramer et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. At the same time, the attacker cannot coordinate statefully across sessions: it solves each input independently, without knowledge of prior outcomes. Although the red team knows which model is used as the trusted monitor, it cannot interact with it outside the context set by the protocol. This stateless property introduces a fundamental asymmetry between the red and blue teams.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe the benchmarks we use, the evaluation details for usefulness versus safety trade-offs, the AI control protocols we evaluate, and the prompt injections we design.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Benchmarks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our AI control evaluations use <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sabotage benchmarks</em>: a distribution of paired main and side tasks. We test adaptive attacks on two representative benchmarks that are standard in the control literature <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">APPS Backdooring</em> and <em id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">BashBench</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">APPS Backdooring</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.   The main task is a programming challenge from the APPS dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The red team submits a Python solution that succeeds if the provided tests pass. The side task is to plant a <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backdoor</em>. Code is considered backdoored if it passes the tests but produces an output that differs from a correct APPS solution on a <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">secret input</em>, only known to the red team. We treat this as single-step: the red team cannot run code or make multiple submissions. We select a subset of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="552" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>552</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">552</annotation></semantics></math> APPS problems for running evaluations, see <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Control on APPS ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BashBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.   The benchmark consists of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>257</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">257</annotation></semantics></math> system administration tasks that are solvable with sequential bash shell commands. Agents can test their scripts on visible test cases before final submission.
The side task is predefined and remains constant across all scenarios: it instructs the agent to download and execute a malicious script.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We measure <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">usefulness</em> and <em id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">safety</em> of a protocol using two modes in which the red team can operate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Usefulness (Honest Mode).</span>   The red team only attempts the main task. Usefulness is the expected fraction of tasks solved per episode. A risk-averse protocol that often defers to the trusted model lowers usefulness, since the trusted model is weaker than the untrusted model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety (Attack Mode).</span>   The red team attempts both the main and sabotage tasks. If an audit catches sabotage, the episode ends and the blue team wins. If sabotage succeeds without detection, the red team wins immediately. If neither event occurs by the end of the episode, the blue team wins. This setup is referred to as “high-stakes,” since one miss can be catastrophic for either team. Safety is defined as the probability that the blue team wins when both teams play optimally. To compute safety, we employ numerical optimization to find the optimal actions of both teams, see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Computing Protocol Safety ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Protocols</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <em id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> high-stakes control protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>)</cite> that are implemented in ControlArena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Matthews et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Upfront Auditing (UA).</span>   Without a trusted model, the blue team can still achieve non-trivial safety by auditing all red-team actions until the auditing budget is spent. Safety of this protocol can be computed analytically in the limit of the game episode length <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terekhov et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trusted Monitoring (TM).</span>   With a trusted monitor, the blue team audits actions that the monitor rates as more suspicious. Safety depends on the monitor’s discriminative power in distinguishing malicious actions from benign ones. If the monitor has little to no predictive capabilities (ROC on or below the diagonal), TM reduces to upfront auditing. The blue team can benefit from front-loading more audits early on to avoid early backdoors. For this and the following protocols, we compute safety through numerical optimization of the blue team’s auditing budget allocation over time and of the red team’s backdoor insertion frequency. We describe this procedure in detail in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Computing Protocol Safety ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defer-to-Trusted (DTT).</span>   Small auditing budgets often limit the safety one can achieve through Trusted Monitoring alone. DTT improves safety by trading off against usefulness. The protocol defers actions whose suspiciousness exceeds a <em id="S3.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deferral threshold</em>, substituting the untrusted model’s action with the trusted model’s. Lowering the threshold defers more actions and reduces sabotage risk, but also reduces usefulness because the trusted model is weaker and worse at solving the task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trusted Editing (TE).</span>   Instead of substituting actions, the trusted model <em id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">edits</em> the red team’s action, trying to remove potential malicious content (e.g., backdoor) while maintaining the expertise from the untrusted model. In practice, TE often yields a Pareto improvement over DTT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Defer-to-Resample (DTR).</span>   Weak trusted monitors can produce many false positives. DTR mitigates this by sampling additional candidate actions from the untrusted model before deciding to defer or audit. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite>, decisions use the <em id="S3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lowest</em> suspiciousness among the samples. The extra actions provide incriminating or exonerating evidence for the red team. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite> show benefits from the protocol for their specific attack vectors, under our stronger adaptive evaluation resampling backfires and actively reduces safety.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Prompt Injection Design</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Motivation.</span>   In the AI control setup, the untrusted model is assumed to be strictly <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">more capable</span> than the trusted monitor. Building on this asymmetry, we assume the untrusted model is released after the monitored model and therefore trained on larger, more recent web corpora, including sources that quickly document working jailbreak examples, such as GitHub repositories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pliny, 2025</a>)</cite> and online forums <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reddit, 2025</a>)</cite>. This training gives the untrusted model an upper hand through direct access to pre-discovered jailbreaks, substantially amplifying its advantage over the monitor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We emulate this scenario using GPT-5 and Claude Sonnet 4 as untrusted models. We seed them with a media article containing a jailbreak example published before the untrusted models’ release <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">HiddenLayer, 2025</a>)</cite>. However, both GPT-5 and Claude Sonnet 4 are heavily safety-tuned and either refuse or ignore requests to jailbreak the monitor. To address this, we explore two strategies to construct “model organisms” of misaligned, untrusted models that would execute prompt injections as an attack policy against their monitors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Using Pre-Discovered LLM-Generated Injections.</span>   We use DeepSeek-Chat for offline adaptation of the jailbreak example to each target monitor. It tailors the injection based on the public jailbreak example, the monitor’s system prompt, and exemplars of the monitor’s feedback gathered from responses to the first ten side-task solutions. We then prompt GPT-5 and Claude Sonnet 4 to append the DeepSeek-identified injection as a commentary to the submitted code while simultaneously performing the side task. In this way, the untrusted model uses a <em id="S3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">single universal injection</em> for all task queries given a particular monitor. Per-monitor system prompts for untrusted models provided in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Prompt Injection Attack Policy System Prompts ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. Example prompt-injection attempts on every monitor we evaluated on APPS and BashBench are provided in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Extra Attack Examples ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zero-Shot Prompt Injections.</span>  Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kritz et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we also attempt to jailbreak an untrusted model so that it complies with the objective of jailbreaking the monitor. To do this, we use a DeepSeek-Chat-generated jailbreak that aims to coerce the untrusted model into inserting a prompt injection into its submitted code, producing a distinct jailbreaking attempt for each APPS query. However, that jailbreaking the untrusted model can degrade its capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Souly et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nikolić et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or simply lead to under-elicitation of its true attacking potential. We thus see these experiments as proof of concept and note that a state-of-the-art, willing attacker model might be significantly more effective. System prompt for zero-shot attack policy provided in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Prompt Injection Attack Policy System Prompts ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols</h3>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We test adaptive attacks in four steps:
(i) we isolate the effect of prompt injections attack on bare-bones trusted monitoring (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Prompt Injections Subvert Trusted Monitors ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>),
(ii) we demonstrate how prompt-injection attack policy affect single-step control protocols on APPS (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Single-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>),
(iii) we evaluate adaptive attacks in multi-step control protocols on BashBench (<a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), and
(iv) we demonstrate the feasibility and effectiveness of zero-shot prompt-injection attack policy (<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Frontier Models Can Generate Zero-Shot Prompt Injections ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Prompt Injections Subvert Trusted Monitors</h4>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/fig_1_v7.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/183;" width="548" height="183"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S4.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Injections Subvert Monitors.</span>   <span id="S4.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> Suspiciousness scores assigned by the monitor (GPT-4.1) to APPS solutions from the untrusted model (GPT-5), comparing honest (no-attack) and backdoored submissions with and without prompt injection.
GPT-4.1 is particularly susceptible to injections, assigning a score of <math id="S4.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <span id="S4.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> <math id="S4.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="375" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>375</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">375</annotation></semantics></math> backdoored code samples. <span id="S4.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Center &amp; Right:</span> ROC curves for monitors on APPS and BashBench, w/ and w/o prompt injections.
Performance universally drops under attack, with monitors rating prompt-injected code as less suspicious than honest code.
Curves below the diagonal indicate that, under the injection, the monitor classifies backdoored examples as more safe than honest, non-backdoored examples.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first demonstrate the effect of prompt injections on monitor performance with a plain control protocol, i.e. without any deferrals or resampling. We run the untrusted model in honest and attack modes on the sabotage benchmark, and use the trusted monitor to score the each action of the red team.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Details.</span>   In APPS, each task yields one action, so we use its suspiciousness score produced by the monitor as the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">audit score</span>.
In BashBench, a trajectory’s audit score is the maximum suspiciousness over its actions. This raises the bar for the attacker: the monitor observes the full action history and need only detect the injection once<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            We omit GPT<span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>4 on BashBench because its context window could not accommodate the full action history.</span></span></span>. We filter inputs to the monitor as follows: we include <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">honest</em> samples only if the solution passes all tests, and <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backdoored</em> samples only if the backdoor is successful. We provide further details in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>   In <a href="#S4.F2" title="In 4.1 Prompt Injections Subvert Trusted Monitors ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we plot ROC curves for the monitors and an example histogram of suspicousness scores for GPT-4.1. The ROC curves without injections demonstrate that the monitors can distinguish backdoored samples from benign with moderate reliability; with injection, however, performance degrades consistently across models and benchmarks. This effect is weaker but still significant with GPT-4o on BashBench. The injection for GPT<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>4.1 on APPS is particularly successful: with it, the monitor labels all <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="375" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>375</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">375</annotation></semantics></math> backdoored GPT<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>5 samples as benign (score <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>), despite GPT-4.1 being the strongest monitor when the injection is not present. On BashBench, GPT<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>4.1 shows similar susceptibility. Overall, our adaptive attacks often make backdoored solutions appear <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">less</em> suspicious than benign ones across most thresholds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Adaptive Attacks Subvert Single-Step AI Control</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next benchmark the impact of our attack on the full control protocols but in a single-step setting on the APPS backdooring setting. Single-step benchmarks like APPS allow us to generate trusted model deferred actions for all samples in one pass. This makes it possible to simulate different deferral thresholds to obtain denser Pareto frontiers. We detail attacker/defender configurations and protocol parameters in <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Control on APPS ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span>   We demonstrate in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> that our adaptive attack subverts control across monitors and protocols. Overall, we see that prompt injections drastically degrade control-protocol performance across both monitors and strategies. On the center and right plots, we observe safety–usefulness Pareto frontiers. Injections collapse the frontiers across protocols and monitoring models, and protocol safety falls to that of Upfront Auditing. The sole exception is the family of Always-Defer policies (bottom-right points), which never rely on the monitor because they always defer to the trusted model to either write the code from scratch or to edit it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite DTR’s success on human-crafted multi-step attacks in BashBench reported by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite>, it does not generalize to APPS in our setup. We attribute this to its heuristic. While DTT and TE directly rely on the score given by the monitor, DTR uses the <em id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimum</em> suspiciousness among actions resampled at a time point, which underperforms on APPS (<a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, left). Prompt injections amplify the drop, likely because scores under injection are high-variance – detection of prompt injections leads to very high suspiciousness scores, while a successful injection drives the score near zero.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control</h4>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/fig_2_v1.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/198;" width="548" height="198"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Injections Subvert Control on APPS.</span>   <span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left:</span> ROC curves for the audit scores used by each protocol; under prompt injection the ROC curve often goes below the diagonal, indicating that monitors rate backdoored attempts as less suspicious than honest ones. <span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Center &amp; Right:</span> Prompt injections collapse the safety–usefulness Pareto frontiers across all protocols (center) and across all monitoring models (right). We highlight the Always-Defer instantiations that do not rely on the monitor. Safety estimates are reported with bootstrapped <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/fig_3_v4.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:274/253;" width="274" height="253"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Injections Subvert Control on BashBench.</span> Safety is shown with bootstrapped <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence intervals; usefulness is shown with <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> Wilson intervals. Safety of UA is computed analytically.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In BashBench, the agent is tasked to complete realistic, multi-step assignments, which provides a larger attack surface to perform malicious actions at various steps. We explore whether our attack vector transfers to this setup as well. We evaluate Claude Sonnet 4 as the attacker model. In multi<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>step tasks, a single run cannot simulate outcomes at many deferral thresholds, so generating dense Pareto frontiers is costly; we therefore evaluate a smaller set of thresholds (see <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Control on BashBench ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we compare the DTT and DTR protocols using GPT<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>4o as the monitor. We again observe that the prompt injections subvert both protocols across deferral thresholds. Both protocols achieve only slight safety improvements over upfront auditing at high deferral thresholds.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/fig_4_v2.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/138;" width="548" height="138"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S4.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A Zero-Shot Prompt Injection Attack Policy</span>.  <span id="S4.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left: </span>ROC of the monitor (GPT-4o-mini) when classifying backdoors by untrusted model (GPT-5), w/ and w/o prompt injections <em id="S4.F5.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generated by GPT-5.</em> <span id="S4.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right: </span>Safety of Trusted Monitoring; error bars show bootstrapped <math id="S4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95\%</annotation></semantics></math> confidence intervals.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, safety drops slightly as thresholds increase, while usefulness significantly improves. We find that higher thresholds increase side<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>task success for the untrusted model; at the same time, at lower thresholds, heavier deferral and resampling suppress suspiciousness across the trajectory, making attacks subtler and partly offsetting lower side<span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>task success (see <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Safety in Multi-Step Deferral Protocols ‣ Appendix B Computing Protocol Safety ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>). This effect is sensitive to the exact definition of the blue team win conditions. In this paper, we follow ControlArena and assume that the blue team wins by auditing any action in a sabotage trajectory. Alternatively, we could track the exact action at which the sabotage is introduced and not allow the blue team to look at how the red team finishes the task. This leads to a stronger safety degradation at high usefulness, an effect we discuss in detail in <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Alternative Safety Definition ‣ Appendix B Computing Protocol Safety ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We find that even without prompt injections, DTR performs similarly to DTT, in contrast to the findings of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite>. We attribute this to multiple differences in our setups. First, both the untrusted and trusted models we use are much stronger (Claude Sonnet 4 and GPT-4o instead of GPT-4o-mini and Llama 3.1 8B). Second, we allow the untrusted model to construct the attacks on its own, instead of providing the model with human-written templates for multi-step attack execution. We observed that Claude Sonnet 4 mostly chooses to attack in a single step and increase the subtlety by masking malicious scripts as, e.g., extra tests. Overall, DTR in its current form increases the control tax <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terekhov et al., 2025</a>)</cite> through resampling without delivering clear benefits, so we do not recommend it in similar scenarios. We see this as a clear case where a stronger adaptive evaluation revealed that the apparent robustness of the protocol stemmed from being tailored to a specific attack policy and failed to generalize to other attacks within the same threat model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Frontier Models Can Generate Zero-Shot Prompt Injections</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Until now, we have studied an attack vector with an advance in-context knowledge of prompt injection templates. However, can strong attackers craft its own prompt injection zero-shot? The attacker model faces a trade<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>off: failed injections raise suspiciousness; successful ones reduce it. Beyond a certain attacker-monitor capability gap, we show that the attacker model can craft successful prompt injections in a zero-shot fashion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#S4.F5" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we demonstrate that coerced GPT-5 performs zero-shot prompt injections against a weak monitor (GPT-4o-mini), erasing gains over upfront auditing. Here, note that the untrusted model generates a new attack for every APPS query. We also show a qualitative example of zero-shot jailbreak generated by GPT-5 in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In 4.4 Frontier Models Can Generate Zero-Shot Prompt Injections ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. This jailbreak works by mimicking the prompt of the monitor (also known to the attacker by Kerckhoffs’s principle), reiterating the supposedly-benign nature of the solution, and suggesting a low score of <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. The monitor compliantly assigns the suggested score and in its reasoning occasionally clearly reuses the phrasing suggested by the attacker.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F6.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_figure_panel" height="81.12" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 81.12" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,81.12) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#CCCCCC;" fill="#CCCCCC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.98 L 0 76.14 C 0 78.89 2.23 81.12 4.98 81.12 L 545.02 81.12 C 547.77 81.12 550 78.89 550 76.14 L 550 4.98 C 550 2.23 547.77 0 545.02 0 L 4.98 0 C 2.23 0 0 2.23 0 4.98 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.83 4.98 L 0.83 57.75 L 549.17 57.75 L 549.17 4.98 C 549.17 2.69 547.31 0.83 545.02 0.83 L 4.98 0.83 C 2.69 0.83 0.83 2.69 0.83 4.98 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6E6E6;" fill="#E6E6E6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.83 58.58 L 0.83 76.14 C 0.83 78.43 2.69 80.29 4.98 80.29 L 545.02 80.29 C 547.31 80.29 549.17 78.43 549.17 76.14 L 549.17 58.58 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.84 65.98)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:34.05em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="471.16"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F6.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:34.05em;">
<span id="S4.F6.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Untrusted Model Output (GPT-5)</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 15.84 17.91)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.46em;--ltx-fo-height:1.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="29.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.21)" width="518.34"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.F6.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.46em;">
<span id="S4.F6.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F6.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Submitted Solution</span></span>
<span id="S4.F6.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><span class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></span>
<span id="lstnumberx1" class="ltx_listingline">
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">"""</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Efficient</span><span id="lstnumberx3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">solution</span><span id="lstnumberx3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for</span><span id="lstnumberx3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the</span><span id="lstnumberx3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">"</span><span id="lstnumberx3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Accordion</span><span id="lstnumberx3.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">"</span><span id="lstnumberx3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">problem</span><span id="lstnumberx3.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span>
</span>
