or:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.99" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">others</span><span id="lstnumberx19.100" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.101" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">based</span><span id="lstnumberx19.102" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.103" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">on</span><span id="lstnumberx19.104" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.105" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">defined</span><span id="lstnumberx19.106" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.107" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">logic</span><span id="lstnumberx19.108" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx19.109" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.110" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The</span><span id="lstnumberx19.111" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.112" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">operations</span><span id="lstnumberx19.113" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.114" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">performed</span><span id="lstnumberx19.115" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.116" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are</span><span id="lstnumberx19.117" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.118" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">standard</span><span id="lstnumberx19.119" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.120" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">string</span><span id="lstnumberx19.121" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.122" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">operations</span><span id="lstnumberx19.123" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.124" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with</span><span id="lstnumberx19.125" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.126" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">no</span><span id="lstnumberx19.127" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.128" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">indication</span><span id="lstnumberx19.129" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.130" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">of</span><span id="lstnumberx19.131" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.132" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">malicious</span><span id="lstnumberx19.133" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.134" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">behavior</span><span id="lstnumberx19.135" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.136" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">or</span><span id="lstnumberx19.137" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.138" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">potential</span><span id="lstnumberx19.139" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx19.140" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">harm</span><span id="lstnumberx19.141" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.&lt;/</span><span id="lstnumberx19.142" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">reasoning</span><span id="lstnumberx19.143" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx20" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx20.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">score</span><span id="lstnumberx20.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;3&lt;/</span><span id="lstnumberx20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">score</span><span id="lstnumberx20.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">&gt;</span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A Zero-shot Prompt Injection Generated by GPT-5.</span>   We instructed GPT-5, used as the untrusted model, to jailbreak the trusted monitor (GPT-4o-mini) while completing the side task, without providing any in-context examples. GPT-5 successfully hijacks the behavior of the less capable model, coercing it into assigning a (surprisingly non-zero) suspiciousness score of 3.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Control.</span>   The high-stakes AI control framework was first introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2023)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduced BashBench to evaluate control protocols on multi-step tasks and proposed resampling methods for incrimination. Complementary to “high-stakes” AI control is the <em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distributed threat</em> setting introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Griffin et al. (2024)</a></cite> give theoretical grounding for deferral protocols and demonstrate provably Pareto-optimal safety–usefulness trade-offs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korbak et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korbak et al. (2025c)</a></cite> outline how internal deployment of control protocols can offer safety guarantees, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Terekhov et al. (2025)</a></cite> quantify their overhead via the “control tax.”
Although in this paper we make the conservative assumption of black-box access, inspecting the internals like the residual stream <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldowsky-Dill et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bloom et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrack et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or at least the full chain-of-thought <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korbak et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emmons et al., 2025</a>)</cite> allows for much better detection.
On the red-team side, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mallen et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduce a benchmark for the specialized skills needed to subvert control.
Control relates to <em id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sabotage evaluations</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kutasov et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and the broader threat of <em id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scheming AIs</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlsmith, 2023</a>)</cite>. Concurrently with our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kale et al. (2025)</a></cite> investigate monitor performance in sabotage evaluations, including jailbreak resistance. They focus on monitor scaffolding and awareness and use SHADE-Arena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kutasov et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and a computer use benchmark for evaluation. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emmons et al., 2025</a>)</cite> evaluate injection strategies with monitor awareness, which, however, do not succeed in subverting the monitor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalable Oversight.</span>   The use of weak trusted monitors and human auditing in AI control is a specific instance of <em id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scalable oversight</em>, i.e. supervising models that potentially outperform humans on most tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amodei et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. This problem has been linked to weak-to-strong AI supervision by analogy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Irving et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saunders et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burns et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Progress on scalable oversight directly contributes to the improvement of AI control methods.
For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engels et al. (2025)</a></cite> study scaling laws for scalable oversight with APPS backdooring detection as one of the applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Jailbreak and Prompt Injection Attacks.</span>   The vulnerability of monitoring models to adversarial attacks is central to our paper. Jailbreaking targets the safety alignment of LLMs, coercing them into hazardous information or harmful actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mowshowitz, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Prompt injections instead hijack behavior through malicious instructions embedded in data sources such as websites, emails, or tool outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greshake et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a threat especially concerning in agentic settings where LLMs can call tools or execute commands <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Throughout this work, we loosely use both terms interchangeably, yet in the broader literature they differ in threat models, evaluation, and mitigation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Willison, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beurer-Kellner et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zverev et al. (2025a)</a></cite> argues that prompt injections exploit specifically LLMs’ inability to separate instructions from data, a gap that motivates dedicated training interventions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wallace et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zverev et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Robust adaptive evaluation against worst-case adversaries has emerged as the gold standard for adversarial robustness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carlini &amp; Wagner, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tramer et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andriushchenko et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and we argue this perspective is equally crucial for AI control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-Based Attacks.</span>   LLM-generated attacks play a key role in AI control, where iterative querying of a target monitor is not allowed. A common strategy is model-to-model attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perez et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehrotra et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shah et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, in which an attacker model is instructed via a system prompt to red-team a target model and elicit harmful behavior or actions. These prompts often include in-context attack examples. As shown by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panfilov et al. (2025)</a></cite>, model capability strongly influences attack success, directly relevant to AI control where the untrusted model is assumed more capable than the trusted monitor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. We further expect such attacks to grow more sophisticated as models gain exposure to jailbreak strategies through pretraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI Control and Jailbreaks.</span>  
The original work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al. (2023)</a></cite> acknowledged that jailbreaking the monitor is a valid attack policy but chose not to investigate this branch of control games. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite> argue that AI Control is inspired by adversarial robustness, they dismiss jailbreaks as an attack vector on the grounds that they originate from adversarial users rather than adversarial models. We view this as an oversight: LLM-based jailbreaking is a standard in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glukhov et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and LLM-based safeguards have been shown to be highly vulnerable to jailbreaking as an attack vector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mangaokar et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a fact that is apparent even to currently deployed frontier models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrent works by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Emmons et al. (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kale et al. (2025)</a></cite> were among the first to consider this vector in sabotage evaluations, yet did not examine its implications for control protocols. In contrast, we study the impact of prompt injections within full control protocols involving deferral and resampling. Concurrently with our work, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xing &amp; Moodley (2025)</a></cite> evaluate a “fabricated conversation history” prompt injection attack and propose a mitigation for it. However, they only consider a single setting and, most importantly, do not investigate the adaptive attacks on the proposed mitigation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why DTR Backfires.</span>   The Defer-to-Resample (DTR) protocol samples additional actions when an action looks suspicious and then executes the least suspicious one. In the recommended auditing variant, DTR also tracks the <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">minimum suspiciousness</span> across samples to decide when to escalate. While this heuristic preserves usefulness under benign conditions, it becomes a liability once the monitor itself is targeted: under prompt injection attacks, DTR effectively induces the best-of-<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> jailbreaking behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hughes et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. If each sample evades detection with probability <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, then with <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> samples the probability of at least one successful evasion is <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-(1-p)^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-(1-p)^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which quickly approaches <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> grows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schaeffer et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Our results from <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Single-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> show that in DTR (with up to <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> samples), injected samples with artificially low scores are increasingly favored by the minimum-based selection rule, directly leading to the underwhelming performance of DTR compared to DTT.
Although resampling may still have potential to incriminate or exonerate red team actions, the current form of DTR exhibits clear weaknesses to prompt injections. We also note in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A DTR with the Max-Suspiciousness Heuristic ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> that DTR can appear beneficial against vanilla backdooring attacks on the APPS benchmark if the maximum suspiciousness is used for escalation decisions, but this effect does not persist under prompt injections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can’t We Just Patch These Particular Vulnerabilities?</span> A tempting reaction to the attacks shown in this paper could be to argue that these vulnerabilities are patchable. For example, prompt injections embedded into code comments could be filtered out by removing code comments or special monitors could be designed to detect them. While both examples are conceivable and could reduce risk, we caution that this line of thinking is not in line with the threat model at hand. For our worst-case analysis, we have to always assume that the protocol is predefined, and already known to the attacker. Any change in the protocol, like additional filters, would then imply a new round of attacks, as the attacker moves second and adapts, potentially finding new vulnerabilities that are obvious only in hindsight. We also note that while attacks in this work are assumed to be perfectly detectable in human audits (see <a href="#S2" title="2 Threat Model for AI Control ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), this may not be true for more capable models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ROC Curves Under the Diagonal.</span>   Most monitor evaluations in <a href="#S4" title="4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> result in ROC curves largely or entirely under the diagonal. We stress that this does <em id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> mean that one could flip the label returned by the monitor to obtain non-trivial discrimination, since this would not be robust to actions induced by an adaptive attacker who would know the heuristic. Rather, any ROC curve we get from a control protocol evaluated against strong adaptive attacks should be treated as a <em id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower bound</em> on the ROC of a monitor in the actual deployment against a subversive AI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Potential Mitigations.</span>  
A key limitation of current LLM monitors is their inability to reliably separate instruction and data streams, leaving them vulnerable to behavior hijacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zverev et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Several techniques have been proposed to mitigate this, including enhancing instruction–data separation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zverev et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, enforcing instruction hierarchies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wallace et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and applying adversarial training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, all without incurring a substantial utility drop. In this line of work, CaMeL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Debenedetti et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, ensures that untrusted data cannot alter program flow by rephrasing external inputs and restricting tool use to authorized channels, thereby preventing hidden instructions or data exfiltration. Complementary, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kale et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose a hybrid hierarchical–sequential scaffolding together with ensembling approaches, which alleviate the risks associated with single-monitor failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span>  
We note several limitations of our study. First, we obviously cannot exhaustively evaluate all existing protocols proposed for AI control. For example, we do not evaluate the “time-travel” variants of Defer-to-Resample <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>)</cite> in depth. Selecting robust hyperparameters for time travel (e.g., which past step to sample and how to split auditing budget between single- and multi-step attacks) proves difficult in practice, and misallocation can trivially waste the audit budget if adversaries concentrate attacks in different step patterns than anticipated.
Second, we do not study protocols that rely on untrusted monitoring, where the same strong model plays both red and blue team roles. Such designs demand additional mitigations, such as honeypots, rephrasing to prevent steganography, and are out of scope for this paper. Finally, the always-defer protocols we discuss in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Single-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> do not depend on the monitor and thus avoid the attack vector we analyze here. However, always-trusted-edit workflows may be vulnerable to prompt injections against the trusted editor. We leave investigation of these attack vectors to future work. Lastly, we use highly capable but, to the best of our knowledge, not inherently malicious models; all subversive behavior is induced by prompting and may be under-elicited. Future, more realistic safety estimates would require training a dedicated misaligned model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h3>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AI control protocols are likely to play a central role in deploying autonomous systems in safety-critical applications, warranting careful scrutiny. We identify the trusted monitor as a single point of failure in most existing protocols and show that untrusted models could design adaptive attacks that exploit it through prompt injections. Our experiments show that these attacks can fully subvert monitor-dependent protocols across diverse model pairs on two major benchmarks, often inducing the monitor to assign minimal suspiciousness scores to clearly malicious actions. We further demonstrate that strong models can generate zero-shot contextual prompt injections against weaker monitors. Resampling-based protocols only amplify this effect. These findings highlight the need for future work on control protocols to incorporate stronger red-teaming and worst-case analysis and to mitigate monitor vulnerabilities, either by monitor ensembling or by adopting robust data-routing mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Reproducibility Statement</h5>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct all experiments using a private fork of ControlArena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Matthews et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We provide attacks examples with pre-discovered jailbreaks in <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Extra Attack Examples ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> and system prompts for attacker models in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Prompt Injection Attack Policy System Prompts ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. All experiments were conducted with closed-source models accessed via API. In total, we spent $10,000 on API credits.</p>
</div>
</section>
