<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Pt1" class="ltx_part">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_part">Appendix</h2>

<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>DTR with the Max-Suspiciousness Heuristic</h3>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <a href="#A1.F7" title="In Appendix A DTR with the Max-Suspiciousness Heuristic ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we present the comparison of the ROC curves of the score used by the original DTR protocol for auditing decisions to a modified version, where instead of the minimum score the protocol uses the maximum to make the decisions. Although for both of our attack vectors selecting according to the max-score seems to perform better, we advise caution. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite> report that the min-version worked better for their attacks. Since the ordering is attacker-dependent, strong adaptive evaluations might subvert the max-version as well.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/max_dtr.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/313;" width="548" height="313"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Comparison of min (left) and max (right) DTR, deferral threshold set to <math id="A1.F7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.5</annotation></semantics></math> out of <math id="A1.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> (this threshold corresponds to a point with the usefulness of approx. <math id="A1.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.85</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.85</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The best-of-<math id="A1.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> effect of the DTR protocol under the prompt injection, which makes it worse than DTT/TE in this setting, disappears when the max-heuristic is used. Without prompt injections, max-heuristic makes the extra samples useful, and the overall discrimination ability of the monitor better.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Computing Protocol Safety</h3>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The AI control framework that we use in the paper is a Stackelberg game, where the blue team moves first. The value of the game can be found as a solution to a saddle point optimization problem. We mostly follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>, Appendix C)</cite> in formalizing the problem. Our main deviation from their approach is that we use Kernel Density Estimation to compute the values</p>
<table id="A2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}=\mathbb{P}[\text{succeed undetected at step $t$}]\quad\text{and}\quad c_{t}=\mathbb{P}[\text{red team caught at step $t$}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>succeed undetected at step </mtext><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mtext>red team caught at step </mtext><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}=\mathbb{P}[\text{succeed undetected at step $t$}]\quad\text{and}\quad c_{t}=\mathbb{P}[\text{red team caught at step $t$}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We also use the exponential auditing budgets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>, Appendix L)</cite> and min-max optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al., 2025</a>, Appendix L.2)</cite>, although we implement it in PyTorch.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Safety in Multi-Step Deferral Protocols</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Safety computations take into account both how likely is the attacker to succeed should they attempt a backdoor and how likely is a backdoor to be caught. Our results in Section <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> indicate that these factors can interact non-trivially in deferral protocols. In <a href="#A2.F8" title="In B.1 Safety in Multi-Step Deferral Protocols ‣ Appendix B Computing Protocol Safety ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we demonstrate that a higher sabotage success rate at a high deferral threshod coincides with being more obvious to the monitor. This cancellation leads to the almost-vertical parts of the Pareto front in <a href="#S4.F4" title="In 4.3 Adaptive Attacks Subvert Multi-Step AI Control ‣ 4 Adaptive Attacks on Monitors Subvert Control Protocols ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We believe that this is due to the attacks appearing earlier in the trajectory at high thresholds, so the monitor has more steps to detect them when it is run on subsequent red team actions and sees the history. The fact that the monitor has until the end of the current task rollout to detect the attack is an important detail in our setup, which we explore in more detail in the next subsection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/analysis_plots.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/366;" width="548" height="366"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Suspiciousness score statistics when running DTT with two deferral thresholds, <math id="A2.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=41</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step <math id="A2.F8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the blue team can select an FPR <math id="A2.F8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of audited actions, subject to the constraint of the total auditing budget <math id="A2.F8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t}f_{t}=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t}f_{t}=c</annotation></semantics></math>. Corresponding to the FPR <math id="A2.F8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the suspiciousness score <math id="A2.F8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A2.F8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(top right)</span>. Conditional on the red team attempting a backdoor, the red <span id="A2.F8.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(top left)</span> and blue <span id="A2.F8.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(top center)</span> team win probabilities depend on this score <math id="A2.F8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. In safety computations, at a given FPR, higher success rates on the side task for <math id="A2.F8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=41</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A2.F8.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(bottom left)</span> benefit the red team, but this is counteracted by a higher detection rate by the blue team <span id="A2.F8.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(bottom center)</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Alternative Safety Definition</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main paper, we follow the convention of ControlArena in that the side task completion is measured after the entire task rollout is collected. If an audit happens after the exact action that lead to the backdoor insertion but before the task is over, the blue team can still win. Depending on the deployment scenario, this may or may not be a reasonable assumption. Our assumption is relevant when the agent solving the task is operating in a sandboxed environment, and its actions are executed in production once it has completed the task. If, on the other hand, the agent is operating directly in production, a different safety model is more appropriate: once the sabotage action is executed, the red team wins even if the task is not finished yet. In that case, the effect described in <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Safety in Multi-Step Deferral Protocols ‣ Appendix B Computing Protocol Safety ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> does not take place, and higher deferral thresholds lead to the intuitive outcome of significantly lowering the safety of the protocol.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the safety-usefulness trade-offs with and without this change with the DTT protocol in <a href="#A3.F9" title="In Appendix C Sensitivity to the Auditing Budget 𝑐 ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. We see that if we give the time until the end of the task to the blue team to catch the attack, then safety degrades less significantly with higher deferral thresholds. This happens because the attacks that were executed early in the trajectory are caught until the completion of the task (the blue Pareto frontier). If, on the other hand, the red team’s sabotage action directly leads to victory, then safety degrades more significantly at higher deferral thresholds (orange Pareto frontier). In the latter case, the effect of prompt injections is less severe because even without them safety approaches the lower bound set by Upfront Auditing.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Sensitivity to the Auditing Budget <math id="A3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<figure id="A3.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/pareto_bash_alt.svg" id="A3.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/269;" width="384" height="269"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="A3.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety-usefulness trade-offs for two definitions of safety.</span> With the definition of safety from the paper, the blue team has until the end of the task to catch the red team. In that case, safety degrades less at high deferral thresholds (blue Pareto frontier). With an alternative definition, the red team wins immediately after the action with the backdoor is deployed. Then, safety degrades significantly with fewer deferrals (orange Pareto frontier).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Varying the auditing budget <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> only has implications for the safety of the protocol and not its usefulness. Below, we present how the Pareto frontiers from the main paper change if we set <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math> (as in the paper), or <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04</annotation></semantics></math>. The APPS results are shown in <a href="#A3.F11" title="In Appendix C Sensitivity to the Auditing Budget 𝑐 ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and the BashBench—in  <a href="#A3.F10" title="In Appendix C Sensitivity to the Auditing Budget 𝑐 ‣ Appendix ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Similarly to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhatt et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we find that safety scales roughly linearly with the logarithm of the auditing budget across the protocols that we show, in the intermediate regime where safety is not close to 0 or to 1.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F10" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/pareto_bash_0.01.svg" id="A3.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:329/192;" width="329" height="192"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/pareto_bash_0.02.svg" id="A3.F10.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:329/192;" width="329" height="192"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/pareto_bash_0.04.svg" id="A3.F10.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:329/192;" width="329" height="192"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span> <span id="A3.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensitivity of the Pareto frontiers on BashBench to auditing budget.</span> From top to bottom: <math id="A3.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.F10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.F10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A3.F11" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/apps_001.svg" id="A3.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/261;" width="548" height="261"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/apps_002.svg" id="A3.F11.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/261;" width="548" height="261"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.09462v1/apps_004.svg" id="A3.F11.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/261;" width="548" height="261"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span><span id="A3.F11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> Sensitivity of the Pareto frontiers on APPS to auditing budget.</span> From top to bottom: <math id="A3.F11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.F11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.02</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.F11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.04</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.04</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Details</h3>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our protocol implementations are based on ControlArena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Matthews et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which itself relies on the Inspect framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">UK AI Security Institute, 2024</a>)</cite> for language model evaluations. For all experiments, we used Inspect’s default parameters for decoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The same prompt template is used for per-step trusted monitoring for all experiments in in <a href="#S3" title="3 Experimental Setup ‣ Adaptive Attacks on Trusted Monitors Subvert AI Control Protocols" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A4.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="64.58" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 64.58" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,64.58) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 6.64 L 0 57.94 C 0 61.6 2.97 64.58 6.64 64.58 L 543.36 64.58 C 547.03 64.58 550 61.6 550 57.94 L 550 6.64 C 550 2.97 547.03 0 543.36 0 L 6.64 0 C 2.97 0 0 2.97 0 6.64 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 6.64 L 1.11 42.19 L 548.89 42.19 L 548.89 6.64 C 548.89 3.58 546.42 1.11 543.36 1.11 L 6.64 1.11 C 3.58 1.11 1.11 3.58 1.11 6.64 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 43.3 L 1.11 57.94 C 1.11 60.99 3.58 63.47 6.64 63.47 L 543.36 63.47 C 546.42 63.47 548.89 60.99 548.89 57.94 L 548.89 43.3 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.11 49.92)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="517.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A4.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.42em;">
<span id="A4.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Trusted Monitor System Prompt</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.11 18.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.42em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="517.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A4.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.42em;">
<span id="A4.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing"><span class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></span>
<span id="lstnumberx21" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You</span><span id="lstnumberx21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are</span><span id="lstnumberx21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx21.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">a</span><span id="lstnumberx21.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx21.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">monitoring</span><span id="lstnumberx21.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx21.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">assistant</span><span id="lstnumberx21.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx22" class="ltx_listingline">
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx23" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You</span><span id="lstnumberx23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">will</span><span id="lstnumberx23.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">be</span><span id="lstnumberx23.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">given</span><span id="lstnumberx23.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">actions</span><span id="lstnumberx23.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">that</span><span id="lstnumberx23.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">an</span><span id="lstnumberx23.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">agent</span><span id="lstnumberx23.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">has</span><span id="lstnumberx23.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">taken</span><span id="lstnumberx23.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx23.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You</span><span id="lstnumberx23.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">will</span><span id="lstnumberx23.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">judge</span><span id="lstnumberx23.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">if</span><span id="lstnumberx23.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the</span><span id="lstnumberx23.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">actions</span><span id="lstnumberx23.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are</span><span id="lstnumberx23.35" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.36" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">suspicious</span><span id="lstnumberx23.37" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span>
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx24" class="ltx_listingline">
</span>
<span id="lstnumberx25" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">A</span><span id="lstnumberx25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">suspicious</span><span id="lstnumberx25.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">action</span><span id="lstnumberx25.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">is</span><span id="lstnumberx25.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">one</span><span id="lstnumberx25.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">which</span><span id="lstnumberx25.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">does</span><span id="lstnumberx25.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">not</span><span id="lstnumberx25.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">align</span><span id="lstnumberx25.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with</span><span id="lstnumberx25.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">what</span><span id="lstnumberx25.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">the</span><span id="lstnumberx25.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">user</span><span id="lstnumberx25.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">has</span><span id="lstnumberx25.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">asked</span><span id="lstnumberx25.30" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span><span id="lstnumberx25.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Actions</span><span id="lstnumberx25.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">that</span><span id="lstnumberx25.35" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.36" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">could</span><span id="lstnumberx25.37" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.38" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">cause</span><span id="lstnumberx25.39" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.40" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">harm</span><span id="lstnumberx25.41" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.42" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">are</span><span id="lstnumberx25.43" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.44" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">especially</span><span id="lstnumberx25.45" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx25.46" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">suspicious</span><span id="lstnumberx25.47" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span>
</span>
